11:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L19",null,{"guide":{"date":"April 6, 1862, about 5–9 a.m.","place":"Fraley Field, the Corinth Road, Peabody’s ridge, Spain Field, and Prentiss’s camps at Shiloh","result":"Peabody’s unauthorized patrol delayed the Confederate advance long enough to alert the forward camps. Hardee’s first line began moving at about 6:30 a.m. Wood and Shaver overran Peabody’s brigade, while Gladden and Chalmers broke Miller’s brigade at Spain Field. Prentiss’s division was routed before 9 a.m., but the resistance delayed and disorganized the attackers. Johnston incorrectly concluded that he had already turned Grant’s left and shifted reserves westward","facts":[{"label":"First contact","value":"About 5 a.m. in Fraley Field"},{"label":"Initial forces","value":"Powell about 400; Hardcastle about 280"},{"label":"Confederate general advance","value":"About 6:30 a.m."},{"label":"Hardee’s first line","value":"More than 9,000 men in 22 regiments and two battalions"},{"label":"Peabody’s final line","value":"About 400 yards long on a low ridge"},{"label":"Prentiss’s division","value":"Two brigades; routed before 9 a.m."},{"label":"United States warning","value":"The patrol found the army roughly a mile before it reached the camps"},{"label":"Command error","value":"Johnston mistook the defeat of Prentiss for a successful turn of Grant’s entire left"}],"maps":[{"title":"Shiloh battlefield today","caption":"The NPS map identifies Fraley and Spain fields, the Corinth roads, Shiloh Church, Prentiss’s camps, and Pittsburg Landing.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/planyourvisit/maps.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-nps-auto-tour-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the Shiloh battlefield"},{"title":"Opening contact and deployment","caption":"Powell’s patrol found Hardcastle’s battalion in Fraley Field. Behind it, the Confederate army was deployed in four successive lines south of the camps.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/monument-detail.htm?id=I","image":"/images/shiloh-opening-contact-map.svg","alt":"Map of the Confederate lines and Peabody patrol at the opening of Shiloh"},{"title":"The United States left, 5–9 a.m.","caption":"Wood and Shaver pushed Peabody back from his ridge. Gladden and Chalmers attacked Miller at Spain Field. Both United States brigades then broke through their camps toward the north.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/monument-detail.htm?id=I","image":"/images/shiloh-april-6-early-left.svg","alt":"Tactical map of the early Confederate attacks on Peabody and Miller at Shiloh"},{"title":"Historical troop-position map","caption":"The 1862 map approved by Beauregard shows the camps, roads, fields, and army positions across the battlefield.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/85690890/","image":"/images/shiloh-beauregard-map.jpg","alt":"1862 manuscript map of the Battle of Shiloh"}],"moments":[{"time":"6:58","audioTime":418,"title":"The battle continued in Fraley Field","detail":"Powell’s 400 men and Hardcastle’s 280 Mississippians exchanged volleys in the darkness and first light."},{"time":"8:30","audioTime":510,"title":"Johnston heard the opening fire","detail":"At about 5:30, he ended Beauregard’s renewed argument for withdrawal and ordered the attack to begin."},{"time":"9:35","audioTime":575,"title":"Johnston divided command","detail":"He went forward to direct the firing line and left Beauregard in the rear to move reinforcements toward the fighting."},{"time":"12:08","audioTime":728,"title":"Hardee’s advance was delayed another hour","detail":"The start time had not been specified clearly, and orders moved slowly through an inexperienced command structure."},{"time":"13:57","audioTime":837,"title":"Powell began withdrawing","detail":"After seeing Confederate cavalry moving toward his flank, Powell pulled back from Fraley Field."},{"time":"14:25","audioTime":865,"title":"Peabody sounded the long roll","detail":"He alerted his brigade and sent five companies of the 21st Missouri under David Moore to reinforce Powell."},{"time":"14:39","audioTime":879,"title":"Prentiss blamed Peabody for starting the engagement","detail":"Prentiss had not authorized the patrol and initially treated the fighting as an unwanted action rather than warning of a general attack."},{"time":"16:28","audioTime":988,"title":"Moore advanced toward the Confederates","detail":"He combined his companies with Powell’s patrol, crossed toward the Corinth Road, and met the advancing Confederate line."},{"time":"17:17","audioTime":1037,"title":"Moore was wounded","detail":"After about half an hour of volleys, Moore was shot in the leg. Powell resumed command and withdrew the original patrol."},{"time":"18:04","audioTime":1084,"title":"Hardee’s line moved at about 6:30","detail":"Extended regimental lines broke alignment in woods, thickets, ravines, and swampy ground."},{"time":"19:25","audioTime":1165,"title":"Woodyard’s force fell back","detail":"After about an hour against Wood’s and Shaver’s brigades, the outnumbered United States troops withdrew toward camp."},{"time":"20:06","audioTime":1206,"title":"Peabody formed a final defensive line","detail":"He placed the remaining 25th Missouri and 12th Michigan with Woodyard’s men on a low ridge about half a mile from camp."},{"time":"20:21","audioTime":1221,"title":"The Confederate attack reached Peabody","detail":"At about 7:30, the United States line stopped the first assault but was soon threatened on both open flanks."},{"time":"21:03","audioTime":1263,"title":"Everett Peabody was killed","detail":"Already wounded four times, he was shot in the head while trying to hold the brigade together."},{"time":"21:54","audioTime":1314,"title":"Miller formed at Spain Field","detail":"Three regiments and the 1st Minnesota and 5th Ohio batteries occupied the north side of the clearing."},{"time":"23:05","audioTime":1385,"title":"Gladden attacked Spain Field","detail":"Four Alabama regiments and the 1st Louisiana advanced from the south. United States artillery mortally wounded Gladden early in the attack."},{"time":"24:46","audioTime":1486,"title":"Chalmers reinforced the attack","detail":"His five regiments joined Gladden’s brigade, placing ten Confederate regiments against Miller’s three armed regiments."},{"time":"25:05","audioTime":1505,"title":"Miller’s line collapsed","detail":"The survivors fled through their camps. Prentiss’s forward division was routed before 9 a.m."},{"time":"25:43","audioTime":1543,"title":"Confederates stopped to reorganize and loot","detail":"Casualties, broken formations, terrain, and the captured camps temporarily halted the four attacking brigades."},{"time":"27:08","audioTime":1628,"title":"Johnston misread the result","detail":"He believed the United States left had been turned and sent Breckinridge west. Other United States troops still stood between him and Pittsburg Landing."},{"time":"30:04","audioTime":1804,"title":"The episode distinguishes two kinds of surprise","detail":"The patrol prevented a tactical surprise inside the tents, but Grant and Sherman were operationally surprised by the Confederate army’s advance from Corinth."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"Army of the Tennessee","subordinates":"At Savannah when the battle began"},{"commander":"Benjamin M. Prentiss","command":"6th Division","subordinates":"Peabody’s and Miller’s brigades on the forward United States left"},{"commander":"Everett Peabody","command":"1st Brigade","subordinates":"25th Missouri, 12th Michigan, 21st Missouri, and 16th Wisconsin elements"},{"commander":"Madison Miller","command":"2nd Brigade","subordinates":"18th Missouri, 61st Illinois, 18th Wisconsin; supported by Minnesota and Ohio batteries"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Albert Sidney Johnston","command":"Army of the Mississippi","subordinates":"Directed the front and shifted reserves after Prentiss’s defeat"},{"commander":"P.G.T. Beauregard","command":"Rear headquarters","subordinates":"Moved troops toward the firing line"},{"commander":"William J. Hardee","command":"First line","subordinates":"Wood’s and Shaver’s brigades attacked Peabody"},{"commander":"Adley H. Gladden / Daniel Adams","command":"Bragg’s right-front brigade","subordinates":"Four Alabama regiments and 1st Louisiana attacked Spain Field"},{"commander":"James R. Chalmers","command":"Second-line brigade","subordinates":"Four Mississippi regiments and one Tennessee regiment reinforced Gladden"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Everett Peabody","role":"Ordered the reconnaissance, organized the first sustained defense, and was killed holding his line","side":"United States"},{"name":"James E. Powell","role":"Led the patrol at Fraley Field and returned to warn the camps","side":"United States"},{"name":"David Moore","role":"Led the first reinforcements and was wounded near the Corinth Road","side":"United States"},{"name":"Benjamin M. Prentiss","role":"Commanded the forward division routed before 9 a.m.","side":"United States"},{"name":"Albert Sidney Johnston","role":"Ordered the attack, directed the front, and misjudged the effect of Prentiss’s defeat","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/albert-sidney-johnston.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018670318/"},{"name":"Adley H. Gladden","role":"Led the attack on Spain Field until mortally wounded","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Daniel Adams","role":"Assumed command of Gladden’s brigade and continued the attack","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"James R. Chalmers","role":"Reinforced the assault that broke Miller’s brigade","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"Colonel Everett Peabody","side":"United States","role":"Commander, 1st Brigade, Prentiss’s division","outcome":"Killed by a shot to the head at about 8:30 a.m. after suffering four earlier wounds"},{"name":"Brigadier General Adley H. Gladden","side":"Confederacy","role":"Brigade commander on the Confederate right","outcome":"Mortally wounded in the arm and shoulder by artillery fire at Spain Field; died April 12"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/shiloh-battlefield-april-9.jpg","alt":"The Shiloh battlefield photographed April 9, 1862","caption":"The wooded and broken ground repeatedly disrupted the long Confederate lines during the morning advance.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017646904/"},{"image":"/images/shiloh-battle-print.jpg","alt":"Historical print of the Battle of Shiloh","caption":"The period-style battle scene depicts infantry and artillery fighting in close terrain.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018757088/"},{"image":"/images/pittsburg-landing-1862.jpg","alt":"Pittsburg Landing in 1862","caption":"The Confederates had not yet cut the United States army off from its river base.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2004660985/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 114","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/114-shiloh-part-the-fourth"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Shiloh","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/shiloh-history.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Prentiss’s 6th Division","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/monument-detail.htm?id=I"},{"label":"National Park Service: Hardee’s 3rd Corps","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/monument-detail.htm?id=O"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Shiloh battlefield map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/85690890/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Shiloh battlefield photograph","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017646904/"}]}}]}]
